{
  "requirement": "A definition glob MUST match a complete full name. `*` MUST match zero or more characters, including dots; all other valid name characters MUST match literally. An empty glob MUST be invalid. Every definition selection MUST use this matching rule, including requirements evidence inputs, CLI definition filters, and the authoring `references` tool, and MUST match against both requirements and named examples.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The sources satisfy `requirements.references.definition_globs`.\n\n**Matching and validation.** `src/definition_glob.rs` is the single matcher.\n- `matches` is a whole-name dynamic-programming match. `*` consumes zero or more bytes, including `.`. Every other byte must equal the name's byte, so the match is anchored at both ends and literal.\n- `validate` rejects an empty glob. It also rejects any character that is not an ASCII alphanumeric, `.`, `_`, `-` or `*`. That is the set `valid_name` in `src/lib.rs` allows, plus the wildcard.\n\n**Every selection path uses it.**\n- Evidence inputs: `InputSelector::resolve` for `Requirements` (`src/inputs.rs`) validates, filters with `definition_glob::matches`, and skips terms. It matches both requirements and examples, and it errors when nothing matches. `add-input --requirements`, evidence validation and the authoring `habeas` tool all go through it. `source_input` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` also uses `matches` to check access, and `resolve` validates afterwards.\n- CLI filters: `status_markdown_with_options` and `info_markdown` in `src/commands.rs` validate and match with the shared function. They return `InvalidInput` for an invalid glob and `NotFound` when nothing matches.\n- Authoring `references` tool: `references` in `src/authoring/tools.rs` validates the selection and uses `matches` for the definition list, the reference sites and the anchor sites. Terms are excluded. Examples are included, since the tool listing shows `requirements.case` (an example).\n- I found no other wildcard or pattern handling for definition names in `src`. All the `{impl}` markings are on the relevant functions.\n\n**Tests.**\n- `src/definition_glob.rs` has a unit test, `wildcard_crosses_dot_segments_and_matches_the_whole_name`.\n  - It covers `*` crossing dot segments.\n  - It checks that a glob does not match a name with an extra prefix, and that a trailing `.*` does not match the bare name.\n  - It checks that an empty glob and one containing `[` are rejected.\n- `tests/commands.rs::commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors` checks that `info` and `status` reject `[` and report \"no definitions match\" for `nothing.*`. It also checks that `requirements.*ready` matches both `requirements.ready` and `requirements.xready`, so `*` matches zero characters, and excludes `requirements.pending`.\n- Other CLI tests cover glob scoping in `status`: `verbose_status_lists_fixme_judgments_and_globs_scope_issue_groups` and `status_preserves_repository_wide_rust_findings_in_every_form`.\n- The `src/authoring/tools.rs` tests exercise `requirements.*` and the invalid selections `\"\"`, `\"bad name\"` and `requirements.missing` in the authoring `references` tool. They also check that examples appear in the results.\n- The evidence-validation test in `src/evidence.rs` uses a `rules.*` input that matches both an example and a requirement.\n\nI found no gaps, no unmarked implementing code, and no behaviour beyond what the requirement specifies. Rejecting the empty glob and unsupported characters is part of the requirement.",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.references.definition_globs",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.references.definition_globs"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} definition_globs",
        "A definition glob MUST match a complete full name. `*` MUST match zero or more characters, including dots; all other valid name characters MUST match literally. An empty glob MUST be invalid. Every definition selection MUST use this matching rule, including requirements evidence inputs, CLI definition filters, and the authoring `references` tool, and MUST match against both requirements and named examples.",
        ":::"
      ]
